#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaf80778-6e4d-52c2-a5db-4a2ef680da77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19650cf966c3c455fa859877b4718c08aea8a343876e5817c625618098a84730.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389065} {:answer/id #uuid "c91635d8-70ba-5406-b235-c716cd8cb5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9dc7559632cbecf450389f362ca478d40576ac9646489fda281519c48f49eb16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389066} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d4992f-3bd8-5933-9744-bd2784689f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/565227489ba6ae7a657a4b2f1e0133bc117e0e666557f58f59ec0272ad8c574e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389067} {:answer/id #uuid "d9925307-ecfb-5b78-8679-f58cb68b9e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} {:answer/id #uuid "f94a3624-0a44-5d1c-a294-b6849858edb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb6908aea50ad1c314efefdaaf922a8c637673a38f76ee8aadcbdc361f44ff78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9925307-ecfb-5b78-8679-f58cb68b9e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4309d7-1981-54e2-82f3-7e4f837d1ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e3c0bea-5d6e-5a25-bcb3-abdcd207f44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160632" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{0}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,1) &  \\\\  & =(1+{v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{1}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+1,\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,0) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59020315-291c-59c3-9224-961256ce3b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416659} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdefaa7-89d2-5114-a2e1-0fce719d5db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/217244e03a7f3eb3d47d936a337de962d680d477ed944a75f7e74d426b53ff8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416660} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5ebdc7-9d12-52cf-8d15-a55e4c96ac2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a60a2532e8cfea8044ddc5e52ef65a8fc83808fc8d35217130d243fe77e8c350.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416661} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcdde3a-a348-5fc5-968e-c1ee5b390866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c1d6e726da49ac8c398357ee00cbb53706a13a6baec25d35a3c594192d804eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416664} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5779ec-3893-54d8-8fd5-cb71c18cf754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/166fff724178c7285d6256c5b6f68c4181b66c246426e75759e196b2f2b3c0a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59020315-291c-59c3-9224-961256ce3b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416659} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b03f791-a25e-5d56-8bc4-3d32145b2ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9cdc646-22ad-53d7-894a-78ecf8a59507" :question/math-academy-id "q-160633" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u(0)) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2},\\,1v) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u(1)) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2},\\,0v) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31e82cf1-a965-5d27-8faf-45515b67a2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416657} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9762f5-d942-5b02-90e1-433937835992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416658} {:answer/id #uuid "758ca0b2-013b-5d8c-a6a5-3ac1ce65e6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416662} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d123e-9efa-5d77-821b-8c45d6d2e441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416663} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c04fe0-8935-5aff-a0db-580177fae6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6c04fe0-8935-5aff-a0db-580177fae6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416666} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47620cc-ff04-5478-975c-18645160652a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aedbf0c6-276b-509d-933d-5c28129e8de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-225139" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2{v}^{2}+u,\\,y=u-{v}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2{v}^{2}+u,\\,u-{v}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+u,\\,u-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2+u,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2+u,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2+t,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/2a/2ac0e0eac9e4633a8d29881782d8dfe93c4c594ca0f99912a98507822c97c4b4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61988fc9-66c2-5bcc-83c1-08b33f8bfdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288717} {:answer/id #uuid "73ab294c-d61b-5d99-813d-6afefa5b3b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288837} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ad950e-b09a-59c9-8006-5f0ea759fde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289112} {:answer/id #uuid "a532b5c7-b1d3-5762-99e5-5ee19c94f559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289131} {:answer/id #uuid "ba18cd8c-2285-5486-8226-bfb496f3b81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1ad950e-b09a-59c9-8006-5f0ea759fde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289112} :answer-field/id #uuid "e191ff6e-214b-558e-9919-97349fa4da47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98c6216e-4e18-5967-be2c-62a3b8d14d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-225160" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=1-u+v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-u+v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-1+v) \\\\  & =(1-{v}^{2},\\,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-{v}^{2},\\,y=v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=1-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=1-x\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2},1-1+0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}-{1}^{0},1-1+1)=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/99/995b106a25173b4e3667fd3fce04f499d7d4a549ae76414328f6e3c406ddd3c6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e97e49e-99a8-5167-95a0-4c3a2aa2b214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288228} {:answer/id #uuid "27c2c113-8813-50c2-98ea-35f094997ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288296} {:answer/id #uuid "370f9d77-be30-5124-88d9-5efd241282bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288404} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0618de-1ff4-56fe-a934-7f77d906a294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288667} {:answer/id #uuid "85690df4-4d70-55f9-838f-5da6f773888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85690df4-4d70-55f9-838f-5da6f773888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288933} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb1a6c5-6f69-563f-88b4-ffb836efd328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "804dcc9f-4962-5beb-bc3d-f0bb08f62de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-225168" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u+1-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u-1,\\,{u}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y={u}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={(x+1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(-1,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to (1+1-2,\\,{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1-2,\\,{0}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(-1,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$\n\n![](images/b1/b139c5b531191bc8aafbb14ad261989ef374503c63f54a567e695e287d4359be.png)"}]}
